"""Grade 7: the New Testament, written as a catechist would plan it."""
R = {
'7-1-1': dict(
 idea='The four Gospels are the heart of all Scripture because they give us the life and teaching of the Incarnate Word. Each evangelist writes for a particular audience with a particular emphasis, yet all four bear witness to the one Christ.',
 hook='Show four photographs of the same building from four sides. “Are they different? Are they all true?” Four Gospels are four faithful views of one Lord.',
 activity='“Four Gospels at a glance” (30 min). Fill a chart with: author, likely audience, symbol (man, lion, ox, eagle), opening, emphasis (Matthew: Jesus fulfills Israel’s story; Mark: the suffering Servant, action; Luke: mercy and the poor; John: the Word, signs and “I am”). Read the opening lines of each (Matthew 1:1, Mark 1:1, Luke 1:1–4, John 1:1).',
 teach=['The Gospels are “the principal source for the life and teaching of the Incarnate Word, our Savior” (CCC 125; Dei Verbum 18). They hold a special place, which is why we stand to hear them at Mass.',
        'There are four Gospels, not one harmonized account, because the Church wanted to keep the fullness of each witness (CCC 127). Matthew, Mark and Luke share much material (the “Synoptics”); John is distinct.',
        'Each tells a historical story shaped by faith. Luke says he “investigated everything carefully” to give certainty (Luke 1:1–4). The Church affirms their historicity (CCC 126; Dei Verbum 19).',
        'The traditional symbols (Revelation 4:7) are Matthew the man, Mark the lion, Luke the ox, John the eagle.'],
 misc=['“The Gospels are biographies in the modern sense.”', '“The differences prove the Gospels are false.”'],
 mem='“These are written so that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that by believing you may have life in his name.” (John 20:31)',
 assess='Ask students to name each Gospel’s focus and one reason the Church keeps four.'),
'7-1-2': dict(
 idea='The Gospels came about in three stages: Jesus’ life and teaching, the oral preaching of the apostles, and the writing down by the evangelists. The evangelists selected, arranged and adapted what they received, always remaining faithful to Jesus.',
 hook='Ask a student to retell a story from last week to a friend; then ask the friend to retell it to another. “What changes? What must stay the same?” Introduce how the Gospel was passed on, with careful memory and living witnesses.',
 activity='“Gospel timeline” (25 min). Create a line: c. AD 30 Jesus’ death and resurrection; 30–50 oral preaching; c. 50–60 Paul’s letters; c. 65–70 Mark; 80–90 Matthew and Luke; c. 90–100 John. Add the three stages. Read Luke 1:1–4 and John 21:24–25 and underline words like “eyewitnesses” and “handed down.”',
 teach=['Three stages (CCC 126; Dei Verbum 19): Jesus’ life and teaching, the apostolic preaching “with that fuller understanding which they enjoyed after being instructed by the glorious events of Christ’s life,” and the written Gospels.',
        'The evangelists “selected certain elements from the many which had been handed on, ... reduced some of them to a synthesis, explained some things in view of the situation of their churches” while keeping the truth of Jesus’ words (CCC 126).',
        'Paul’s letters (written before the Gospels) already hand on core traditions: the Eucharist (1 Corinthians 11:23–26), the Resurrection (1 Corinthians 15:3–8).',
        'Comfortable with the human side of the process, remind students that the Spirit guided it (CCC 105–107).'],
 misc=['“Nobody wrote anything down until much later, so we can’t trust it.”', '“The evangelists made up stories to fit their message.”'],
 mem='“We did not follow cleverly devised myths... but we were eyewitnesses of his majesty.” (2 Peter 1:16)',
 assess='Ask students to place the three stages in order and say why oral tradition mattered.'),
'7-1-3': dict(
 idea='The Church gives three guidelines for reading Scripture: attend to the unity of the whole Bible, read it within the living Tradition of the Church, and respect the analogy of faith. With prayer and in context, the Gospels speak today.',
 hook='Show a puzzle with one piece alone. “Does this piece make sense without the picture?” A verse alone can mislead.',
 activity='“Three tools” (30 min). Take Luke 24:44–48 and a short passage (e.g., Matthew 16:13–19). Students use the three tools: (1) unity: which Old Testament texts or other Gospel passages relate? (2) Tradition: how have the Fathers or the liturgy read it? (3) analogy of faith: how does it fit with what the Church teaches? Report to the class.',
 teach=['CCC 112–114: be especially attentive to “the content and unity of the whole Scripture,” read it “within the living Tradition of the whole Church,” and be attentive to “the coherence of the truths of faith among themselves” (analogy of faith).',
        'Also ask: who wrote it, to whom, when, and in what literary form (CCC 109–110). Context prevents misuse of single verses.',
        'Prayer matters: “Scripture must be read and interpreted in the sacred spirit in which it was written” (CCC 111). Lectio divina is the Church’s ancient practice.',
        'Show a common misreading (e.g., “do not judge,” Matthew 7:1) and how the three tools correct it.'],
 misc=['“Anyone can interpret the Bible however they feel.”', '“Context is a way to explain the Bible away.”'],
 mem='“Then he opened their minds to understand the Scriptures.” (Luke 24:45)',
 assess='Ask students to use the three tools on a short passage you provide.'),
'7-2-1': dict(
 idea='“The Word became flesh and dwelt among us.” Jesus is true God and true man: one divine Person with two natures, fully divine and fully human. John’s prologue states it beautifully.',
 hook='Ask, “If you wanted to show someone who you are, what is the best way?” Show a picture and then show yourself. God did the second: he came himself in Jesus.',
 activity='“Prologue in pieces” (30 min). Read John 1:1–18 in parts. Students highlight key words (Word, with God, was God, flesh, light, grace and truth). Then explain the prologue in a 60-second talk to a partner, using the terms person and nature.',
 teach=['The Word (Logos) was in the beginning, with God and was God, and became flesh (John 1:1, 14; CCC 461–463). He did not cease to be God.',
        'The Council of Chalcedon (451) taught: one Person in two natures, without confusion, change, division or separation (CCC 464–469). Jesus has a human body, soul, mind and will.',
        'Heresies to name: Arianism (Jesus is a creature), Docetism (he only seemed human), Nestorianism (two persons), Monophysitism (one nature). Each was condemned (CCC 465–467).',
        'The Incarnation is the source of the sacraments: God uses material things because God became matter.'],
 misc=['“Jesus was God pretending to be man.”', '“Jesus became divine after his baptism.”'],
 mem='“And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us, full of grace and truth.” (John 1:14)',
 assess='Ask students to explain “one Person, two natures” and name one heresy it rules out.'),
'7-2-2': dict(
 idea='Luke and Matthew tell the infancy of Jesus from different perspectives: Luke from Mary’s viewpoint with angels and shepherds, Matthew from Joseph’s with the magi. Together they reveal Jesus as Son of God, Son of David and Savior of all nations.',
 hook='Say: “Imagine telling the story of a birth from the mother’s viewpoint, then from the father’s.” That is what Luke and Matthew do.',
 activity='“Two infancy narratives” (30 min). Students compare Matthew 1–2 and Luke 1–2 in a two-column chart: who receives the message, who visits, what Old Testament texts are cited, what is the mood. Identify shared facts: Bethlehem, virgin birth, Joseph of David’s line, Nazareth.',
 teach=['The infancy narratives reveal Jesus’ identity (CCC 496–507, 525–530). Luke has the Annunciation, Visitation, the shepherds, Simeon and Anna; Matthew has the dream of Joseph, the magi, Herod and the flight to Egypt.',
        'Mary’s Magnificat (Luke 1:46–55) sings of a God who lifts the lowly. The magi show that Gentiles come to worship (Epiphany; CCC 528).',
        'The virginal conception is taught in both Gospels (CCC 496–498). Jesus is born in Bethlehem, the city of David (Micah 5:2).',
        'Differences in detail are not contradictions but complementary views. Avoid forcing a mixed account.'],
 misc=['“The Christmas story is a fairy tale.”', '“Three kings arrived at the manger on December 25.”'],
 mem='“My soul magnifies the Lord, and my spirit rejoices in God my Savior.” (Luke 1:46–47)',
 assess='Ask students to name three details unique to Luke, three unique to Matthew and one shared by both.'),
'7-2-3': dict(
 idea='For about thirty years Jesus lived in Nazareth, obeying Mary and Joseph, learning a trade and praying. This “hidden life” makes ordinary life holy. Luke gives us one glimpse at twelve, when Jesus stays in the Temple.',
 hook='Ask: “What do we know about Jesus’ first thirty years?” (almost nothing). “Why would God spend most of his time on earth in ordinary life?”',
 activity='“A day in Nazareth” (30 min). Using historical background (village life, carpentry, synagogue school, family prayer), students write a short diary entry for a day in the Holy Family’s home. Include prayer and work. Read Luke 2:41–52.',
 teach=['The hidden life (CCC 531–534): Jesus obeyed, worked and prayed. He sanctified family life and work (CCC 532).',
        'Joseph, the just man, guarded and provided (Matthew 1:19). Mary kept all these things in her heart (Luke 2:51). Jesus was “subject to them” (CCC 532).',
        'At twelve he said, “I must be in my Father’s house” (Luke 2:49): the first words of Jesus in the Gospel show his divine mission.',
        'Link to Catholic teaching on the dignity of work and the importance of family prayer.'],
 misc=['“Nothing important happened in Jesus’ youth.”', '“Jesus did not need to learn.”'],
 mem='“And Jesus increased in wisdom and in stature, and in favor with God and man.” (Luke 2:52)',
 assess='Ask students to explain why the hidden life matters for ordinary people.'),
'7-3-1': dict(
 idea='At the Jordan, Jesus is baptized, the heavens open, and the Father and the Spirit identify him as the beloved Son. In the desert he faces the same temptations that Israel and Adam failed, and he overcomes them.',
 hook='Ask: “Why would a sinless man need baptism?” Then listen to Matthew 3:13–17. Jesus joins us; he shares our life so that he can lift it.',
 activity='“Three temptations” (30 min). Match each temptation (stones to bread, jump from the Temple, kingdoms of the world) with an Israel failure in the desert (manna, testing God, idolatry) and with a modern parallel (comfort, showing off, power). Note which scripture Jesus quotes each time (Deuteronomy 8:3; 6:16; 6:13).',
 teach=['The baptism of Jesus reveals the Trinity: the Father’s voice, the Spirit as a dove, the Son (CCC 535–537). It anticipates our own Baptism and his Passion.',
        'The temptation in the desert recalls Israel’s forty years and Adam’s fall (CCC 538–540). Jesus resists with the Word of God.',
        'He is the new Adam and the new Israel. Lent follows these forty days (CCC 540).',
        'Temptation is not sin. Jesus “has been tempted as we are, yet without sin” (Hebrews 4:15).'],
 misc=['“Jesus needed to be baptized for his sins.”', '“Temptation means you have already sinned.”'],
 mem='“Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God.” (Matthew 4:4)',
 assess='Ask students to explain what the baptism and temptation reveal about Jesus.'),
'7-3-2': dict(
 idea='Jesus proclaimed the Kingdom of God, which has come near in his person. He taught in parables, short stories from daily life that reveal and conceal, inviting listeners to decide.',
 hook='Tell a short parable of your own (e.g., a lost phone found with great joy). Ask what it means. “Jesus told stories like this to make people think.”',
 activity='“Interpret three parables” (30 min). Groups take one parable each: sower (Mark 4:1–20), mustard seed and leaven (Matthew 13:31–33), lost sheep or prodigal son (Luke 15). Students retell it, name the surprise, and state what it says about the Kingdom and about us.',
 teach=['The Kingdom of God is the reign of God in Christ, already present and still to come (CCC 541–546, 2816). Jesus invites all to enter, especially the poor and sinners.',
        'Parables use ordinary images (seeds, coins, sheep) to reveal what God is like. They also call for a response: “He who has ears to hear, let him hear” (Mark 4:9; Matthew 13:10–17).',
        'Key parables: sower, weeds, mustard seed, leaven, treasure, pearl, net, lost sheep, lost coin, prodigal son, good Samaritan.',
        'Resist reducing parables to morals; look for the shock of grace.'],
 misc=['“Parables are only nice stories.”', '“The Kingdom of God is only heaven after death.”'],
 mem='“The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took and sowed in his field.” (Matthew 13:31)',
 assess='Ask students to retell a parable and explain what it reveals about God’s Kingdom.'),
'7-3-3': dict(
 idea='Jesus’ miracles are signs of the Kingdom. They show his power over nature, sickness, demons and death, they accompany his word, and they call for faith. They are not magic or show.',
 hook='Read John 2:1–11. Ask: “Why did John call the water into wine a ‘sign’?” Signs point beyond themselves.',
 activity='“Classify the signs” (30 min). Sort miracles into categories: nature (calming the storm), healing (blind man), exorcism (Gerasene demoniac), raising the dead (Lazarus). For each, name what it reveals about Jesus and what response it asks.',
 teach=['Miracles accompany his words and show the Kingdom’s arrival (CCC 547–550). Jesus does not remove all evil, but frees people from it as a sign of his saving mission.',
        'They call for faith: “Your faith has made you well” (Mark 5:34). Jesus refuses to work signs to satisfy curiosity (Matthew 12:38–39).',
        'John calls them “signs” and uses them to lead to deeper truths: the bread of life, the light of the world, the resurrection and the life.',
        'Cana (John 2) also shows Mary’s intercession and the transformation of marriage (CCC 1613).'],
 misc=['“Miracles are legends.”', '“God must heal everyone who prays.”'],
 mem='“Do whatever he tells you.” (John 2:5)',
 assess='Ask students to choose one miracle, say what it reveals about Jesus and what response it asks.'),
'7-4-1': dict(
 idea='The Beatitudes are Jesus’ portrait of the way to true happiness. They begin the Sermon on the Mount, are promises as much as challenges, and describe Jesus himself.',
 hook='Poll the class: “What do people say make you happy?” Then read Matthew 5:3–12 and ask which are surprising.',
 activity='“Beatitudes memory and reflection” (30 min). Students memorize the eight, then pair each with a saint or contemporary example and with one practical choice. Silent reflection: which Beatitude challenges me most?',
 teach=['The Beatitudes (Matthew 5:3–12; Luke 6:20–26) are the heart of Jesus’ preaching (CCC 1716). They fulfill the promises made to Abraham and point to the Kingdom.',
        'They describe the vocation of the faithful: share Christ’s Passion and Resurrection (CCC 1717). Each blessing has a reward now and later.',
        '“Poor in spirit” means depending on God. “Pure in heart” means undivided love. “Meek” is strength under control.',
        'They answer the desire for happiness that God has placed in every heart (CCC 1718–1719).'],
 misc=['“The Beatitudes are impossible ideals.”', '“Being poor in spirit means low self-esteem.”'],
 mem='“Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.” (Matthew 5:3)',
 assess='Ask students to recite the eight Beatitudes and explain one in their own words.'),
'7-4-2': dict(
 idea='Jesus did not abolish the Law but fulfilled it, going to the heart: not only “do not kill” but do not hate; not only “do not commit adultery” but do not lust. “You have heard… but I say to you.”',
 hook='Ask: “Is it enough to avoid hitting someone? What about words?” Read Matthew 5:21–22.',
 activity='“Antitheses” (30 min). Students fill a chart with six antitheses from Matthew 5 (anger, adultery, divorce, oaths, retaliation, enemies): the old rule, Jesus’ deepening, and a modern application. Discuss why the demand is high and what gives us the strength (grace).',
 teach=['Jesus “fulfills” the Law (Matthew 5:17; CCC 577–582). He is the only one who kept it perfectly and he gives its inner meaning.',
        'The New Law is a law of love and grace (CCC 1965–1974). The Sermon on the Mount gives its charter. “Be perfect, as your heavenly Father is perfect” (Matthew 5:48).',
        'The Church distinguishes moral, ceremonial and judicial precepts (CCC 1961–1964). The moral law remains; ceremonial law is fulfilled in Christ.',
        'Interpret hard sayings (e.g., plucking out an eye) as strong ways of urging seriousness about sin.'],
 misc=['“Jesus abolished the Old Testament law.”', '“Following Jesus is just being nice.”'],
 mem='“Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them but to fulfill them.” (Matthew 5:17)',
 assess='Ask students to restate two antitheses and explain how Jesus deepens the law.'),
'7-4-3': dict(
 idea='Jesus teaches three practices of devotion: almsgiving, prayer and fasting. He warns us to do them for God, not for show. The Our Father sits at the center. These practices are the traditional works of Lent.',
 hook='Ask: “Would you rather be applauded or be quietly true?” Read Matthew 6:1–4.',
 activity='“Lenten plan” (30 min). Students plan a realistic Lent with one practice in each category: prayer (daily time), fasting (from a food, media or a habit), almsgiving (money or time for others). They discuss how to keep it hidden. Read Matthew 6:1–18.',
 teach=['Almsgiving, prayer and fasting are ways to turn back to God (CCC 1434–1438). They are the Church’s penitential practices.',
        'Jesus insists on secrecy of heart: “Your Father who sees in secret will reward you” (Matthew 6:4). Hypocrisy empties religion.',
        'He gives the Our Father as the model of prayer (Matthew 6:9–13; CCC 2759–2776). Fasting trains us to hunger for God.',
        'Church law: Ash Wednesday and Good Friday are days of fast and abstinence; Fridays of Lent are days of abstinence (CCC 2043).'],
 misc=['“Giving up chocolate for Lent is enough.”', '“Public worship is hypocrisy.”'],
 mem='“Your Father who sees in secret will reward you.” (Matthew 6:6)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the three practices and why Jesus says to do them in secret.'),
'7-5-1': dict(
 idea='Jesus entered Jerusalem as the humble king and then gave himself at the Last Supper. In the bread and wine he gave us his Body and Blood. This is the start of the Eucharist.',
 hook='Ask: “If you knew it was your last night with your friends, what would you do?” Read Luke 22:7–20.',
 activity='“Upper room” (30 min). Draw or build the setting of the Last Supper using Luke 22 and Passover customs. Mark the moments: washing of feet (John 13), the bread, the cup, “Do this in memory of me,” the new commandment. Connect to the Mass parts.',
 teach=['The entry into Jerusalem fulfills Zechariah 9:9 (humble king on a donkey) and the crowds cry “Hosanna” (CCC 557–560). Palm Sunday begins Holy Week.',
        'At the Last Supper Jesus instituted the Eucharist (CCC 1337–1340). “Do this in memory of me” gives the apostles and their successors the command.',
        'The Last Supper was a Passover meal; the Eucharist is the new Passover (CCC 1340, 1363). John adds the foot washing and the new commandment (John 13:34).',
        'On Holy Thursday the Church celebrates the institution of the Eucharist and the priesthood.'],
 misc=['“Palm Sunday is a happy day only.”', '“The Last Supper was only a farewell dinner.”'],
 mem='“This is my body, which is given for you. Do this in remembrance of me.” (Luke 22:19)',
 assess='Ask students to retell the Last Supper and explain two ways it connects to the Mass.'),
'7-5-2': dict(
 idea='In Gethsemane Jesus prayed in agony; he was arrested, tried, scourged and crucified. He freely accepted the Father’s will. The Passion shows both the depth of human sin and of God’s love.',
 hook='Read Luke 22:39–46. Ask: “Why would Jesus ask the disciples to stay awake?” Jesus is truly human and needs companions.',
 activity='“Passion in parts” (35 min). Read Luke 22–23 in scenes with roles. After each scene, freeze and write a one-line reflection (“What does this show about Jesus? About us?”). Close with a few minutes of silence.',
 teach=['In Gethsemane Jesus entrusts himself to the Father: “Not my will but yours” (Luke 22:42). His agony shows his humanity and obedience (CCC 612).',
        'The trials before the Sanhedrin and Pilate show injustice. The responsibility is not on one people: all sinners are the cause (CCC 597–598).',
        'On the Cross he forgives (“Father, forgive them”), promises paradise to the thief, and entrusts his mother (Luke 23:34, 43; John 19:26–27).',
        'Treat the Passion reverently. Keep clear that his death was a free act of love (CCC 609).'],
 misc=['“The Jews are responsible for Jesus’ death.”', '“Jesus did not know what would happen.”'],
 mem='“Father, into your hands I commit my spirit!” (Luke 23:46)',
 assess='Ask students to name key moments and explain what each shows about Jesus’ freedom and love.'),
'7-5-3': dict(
 idea='Why did Jesus have to die? The New Testament offers images: ransom, sacrifice, expiation, reconciliation, the Suffering Servant bearing our sins. All point to love: “Greater love has no one than this.”',
 hook='Ask: “If you owed a debt you couldn’t pay, and someone paid it, how would you feel?” Use as an image, not a full explanation.',
 activity='“Reflection and images” (30 min). Read Isaiah 53 and 1 Peter 2:24. Students list the images (ransom, sacrifice, healing, lamb). Choose the one that speaks most and write a reflection: “For me, the Cross means…” Share a few.',
 teach=['Christ died “for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures” (1 Corinthians 15:3; CCC 599–605). The Father did not demand the death of his Son as a cruel price; the Son freely offered himself in love (CCC 603–605).',
        'Images: redemption (ransom), expiation, reconciliation, satisfaction (CCC 613–618). No single image exhausts the mystery.',
        '“By his wounds you have been healed” (1 Peter 2:24). The Cross is the sacrifice that Mass makes present (CCC 1366).',
        'The Cross is also the way for us: “take up your cross” (CCC 618).'],
 misc=['“God the Father punished Jesus because he was angry.”', '“Jesus’ death was an accident.”'],
 mem='“Greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends.” (John 15:13)',
 assess='Ask students to explain two images for the meaning of the Cross and avoid the misunderstanding that the Father is cruel.'),
'7-6-1': dict(
 idea='On the third day the tomb was empty and Jesus appeared to many: to Mary Magdalene, to the apostles, to Thomas, to the disciples at Emmaus. He rose in a real, glorified body.',
 hook='Say: “If something this big happened, what evidence would you expect?” (an empty tomb, eyewitnesses, changed lives). Introduce the accounts.',
 activity='“Compare the accounts” (30 min). Read the Resurrection narratives in Luke 24 and John 20. Make a table: who came to the tomb, who appeared, what was said, what Jesus did (ate, showed wounds). Note shared details and variations. Discuss Thomas.',
 teach=['The empty tomb and the appearances are the foundation of the Church’s faith (CCC 639–644). Women were first witnesses, remarkable for the culture.',
        'Jesus rose in a real body, not a resuscitation or a ghost: he ate (Luke 24:42–43) and bore wounds (John 20:27), yet he passes through doors and is not at once recognized. His body is glorified (CCC 645–646).',
        'Paul lists witnesses, including 500 at once (1 Corinthians 15:5–8). The apostles were willing to suffer and die for what they proclaimed.',
        'Handle “proofs” carefully: the Resurrection is historical and also beyond history (CCC 647).'],
 misc=['“The disciples hallucinated.”', '“Resurrection means his spirit lives on.”'],
 mem='“My Lord and my God!” (John 20:28)',
 assess='Ask students to name three appearances and explain what they show about the risen body.'),
'7-6-2': dict(
 idea='The Resurrection confirms that Jesus is God and that his teaching is true. It accomplishes our justification and new life, and it promises our own resurrection. “If Christ has not been raised, your faith is in vain.”',
 hook='Say: “Imagine a team that has been told it has already won.” The Resurrection is the sign that evil has lost.',
 activity='“Creed statement” (25 min). Read 1 Corinthians 15:1–22 aloud. Students write a three-sentence creed on the Resurrection: what happened, what it proves, what it promises. Compare with the Creed’s words.',
 teach=['Paul: “If Christ has not been raised, your faith is futile” (1 Corinthians 15:17). The Resurrection is the “crowning truth of our faith” (CCC 638).',
        'It confirms Jesus’ divinity and teaching (CCC 651–653), accomplishes our justification and new life (CCC 654), and is the pledge of our own resurrection (CCC 655).',
        'Sunday is the weekly Easter. The Easter Vigil is the “mother of all vigils.” Baptism is a sharing in Christ’s death and resurrection (Romans 6:4).',
        'The Resurrection answers the problem of death. Death is not the last word.'],
 misc=['“Easter is a spring festival.”', '“We will become angels or ghosts.”'],
 mem='“Christ has been raised from the dead, the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep.” (1 Corinthians 15:20)',
 assess='Ask students to explain three effects of the Resurrection.'),
'7-6-3': dict(
 idea='Jesus ascended to the Father and sent the Holy Spirit at Pentecost. The apostles, filled with courage, began the Church’s mission “to the ends of the earth.”',
 hook='Ask: “What would you do if your leader left and said, ‘Wait for a gift’?” Read Acts 1:6–11.',
 activity='“Map the mission” (30 min). Using Acts 1:8 as outline (Jerusalem, Judea and Samaria, the ends of the earth), students plot the spread on a map: Pentecost, Samaria, Ethiopia, Antioch, Asia Minor, Greece, Rome. Add key people.',
 teach=['The Ascension is Christ’s enthronement and the pledge of our own ascent (CCC 659–667). He remains with us through the Church and the sacraments.',
        'At Pentecost the Spirit comes with wind and fire; the apostles speak in many languages and Peter preaches (Acts 2). The Church is “born” (CCC 731–732, 767).',
        'The Church’s mission is universal: “Go and make disciples of all nations” (Matthew 28:19). Acts shows the Spirit guiding it.',
        'The Spirit is still active. Invite students to name where.'],
 misc=['“The Ascension means Jesus left us.”', '“Pentecost was a one-time event.”'],
 mem='“You will receive power when the Holy Spirit has come upon you, and you will be my witnesses.” (Acts 1:8)',
 assess='Ask students to trace Acts 1:8 on a map and describe Pentecost.'),
'7-7-1': dict(
 idea='The first Christians “devoted themselves to the apostles’ teaching and fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers.” They shared their goods, prayed in the Temple and at home, and grew day by day.',
 hook='Ask: “What would a team that really loved each other look like?” Read Acts 2:42–47.',
 activity='“Compare with our parish” (25 min). Draw a four-part chart: teaching, fellowship, breaking of bread, prayers. In each, list what our parish does and one thing we could do better. Choose one for the class to try.',
 teach=['Acts 2:42–47 and 4:32–35 describe a community of teaching, fellowship (koinonia), Eucharist and prayer (CCC 949, 2624–2625).',
        'They shared goods voluntarily: the root of Catholic charity and the ideal of solidarity (CCC 952).',
        'The pattern is the Mass: Word (teaching), communion, bread, prayer. Even then the Church had leaders: the apostles and deacons (Acts 6:1–6).',
        'There were conflicts and failures (Ananias and Sapphira, Acts 5), a reminder that the Church is holy and made of sinners.'],
 misc=['“The early Church had no leaders.”', '“Everything was perfect then.”'],
 mem='“They devoted themselves to the apostles’ teaching and the fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers.” (Acts 2:42)',
 assess='Ask students to name the four marks of the early community and compare them with the Mass.'),
'7-7-2': dict(
 idea='Peter, the rock, led the Church, opened it to Gentiles (Cornelius) and was the first Pope. Paul, once a persecutor, met Christ on the road to Damascus and became the apostle to the Gentiles, traveling the Mediterranean.',
 hook='Ask: “Who is the most unlikely person you can imagine becoming a great missionary?” Read Acts 9:1–19.',
 activity='“Map Paul’s journeys” (30 min). Trace Paul’s three missionary journeys and voyage to Rome. Mark Damascus, Antioch, Philippi, Corinth, Ephesus, Athens, Rome. Add Peter’s story in Acts 10 (Cornelius) at Caesarea. Compare their roles.',
 teach=['Jesus gave Peter the keys: “You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church” (Matthew 16:18–19; CCC 551–553). Peter leads the apostles and the Church and confirms his brethren (Luke 22:32).',
        'In Acts 10 Peter baptizes the Gentile Cornelius after a vision and the Spirit’s coming, showing the Church is for all (CCC 849–856).',
        'Paul’s conversion (Acts 9) shows grace transforming a persecutor. His journeys planted churches. Both Peter and Paul were martyred in Rome (around AD 64–67). The Church celebrates them on June 29.',
        'The Pope today is the successor of Peter (CCC 882).'],
 misc=['“Paul founded Christianity, not Jesus.”', '“Peter was just one apostle among equals.”'],
 mem='“You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church.” (Matthew 16:18)',
 assess='Ask students to explain Peter’s role, Paul’s role and how they worked together.'),
'7-7-3': dict(
 idea='When Gentile converts raised the question of circumcision, the apostles and elders met in Jerusalem (Acts 15). Led by Peter and James and guided by the Spirit, they decided: Gentiles need not follow the whole Law. This is the model for Church councils.',
 hook='Ask: “How should a group settle a serious disagreement?” Read Acts 15:1–2 and the verdict in 15:28: “It has seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us.”',
 activity='“Role-play the council” (30 min). Assign roles: Pharisee believers, Paul and Barnabas, Peter, James, an observer. Act the debate and the decision. Then write the letter (Acts 15:23–29). Discuss how this relates to councils like Nicaea.',
 teach=['The Council of Jerusalem (c. AD 49) settled whether Gentiles must be circumcised (Acts 15). It combined testimony, Scripture and decision (CCC 865, 880–892).',
        'Peter speaks first and definitively; James proposes a pastoral solution; the whole group agrees. “It has seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us” shows the Church’s authority (CCC 882–892).',
        'It is the first ecumenical-style council. The Church has held 21 councils, including Nicaea (325), Trent (1545) and Vatican II (1962).',
        'The Church’s unity is lived through bishops with the Pope.'],
 misc=['“Church councils invented doctrines.”', '“Everyone in the Church decides by vote.”'],
 mem='“It has seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us.” (Acts 15:28)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the question, who decided and how the decision was reached.'),
'7-8-1': dict(
 idea='Paul’s letters explain what Jesus’ death and resurrection mean. In Romans he teaches that we are justified by grace through faith, a faith that works through love, and that the Spirit makes us God’s children.',
 hook='Ask: “Can you earn a gift?” (No; then it’s a wage.) Read Romans 5:1–11 and Ephesians 2:8–9. Grace is a gift.',
 activity='“Outline Romans” (30 min). Students outline chapters 5–8: justified by faith (5), dead to sin and alive in Christ (6), struggle with sin (7), life in the Spirit (8). For each, write a one-line summary and a key verse (5:8, 6:4, 7:24–25, 8:38–39).',
 teach=['Justification is God’s gift: forgiveness and new life by grace through faith in Christ (CCC 1987–1995). It is not earned by works, yet faith “works through love” (Galatians 5:6; CCC 1814, 2010).',
        'Catholic teaching: grace initiates, faith responds, and good works are the fruit and also meritorious by God’s grace (CCC 2006–2011). The Church affirms that Paul and James agree (James 2:24).',
        'In Romans 8 Paul describes life in the Spirit: adoption as children, nothing can separate us from God’s love (CCC 1996–1999).',
        'Avoid caricaturing Protestant views; the Joint Declaration on Justification (1999) shows convergence.'],
 misc=['“Catholics think you earn salvation by works.”', '“Faith alone means works don’t matter.”'],
 mem='“For I am sure that neither death nor life… will be able to separate us from the love of God in Christ Jesus our Lord.” (Romans 8:38–39)',
 assess='Ask students to explain justification by grace and the role of faith and works.'),
'7-8-2': dict(
 idea='The letters give practical guidance for Christian life: “put on compassion, kindness, humility, meekness and patience, bearing with one another and forgiving.” Paul calls the Church to unity, holiness, and love in family and community.',
 hook='Ask: “If you were writing a letter to your parish about how to treat each other, what would you say?” Read Colossians 3:12–17.',
 activity='“Clothing list” (25 min). Students make a “wardrobe of virtues” from Colossians 3 and Ephesians 4–6 (compassion, kindness, humility, meekness, patience, forgiveness, love, peace, gratitude). For each write a real situation at school or home where they will “put it on.”',
 teach=['Paul’s letters combine doctrine and morality: “you have put off the old nature… and put on the new” (Colossians 3:9–10). Baptism changes how we live.',
        'He teaches about the Church (Ephesians 4:1–6: one body, one Spirit, one Lord) and about households (Ephesians 5–6), always “in Christ.”',
        'The fruits of the Spirit (Galatians 5:22–23) are the sign of this life (CCC 1832).',
        'Note that some texts (e.g., on slaves or women) have been misused. The Church reads them in light of the whole Gospel and human dignity (CCC 1935).'],
 misc=['“Paul’s moral teaching is optional.”', '“Christian life is only about avoiding sin.”'],
 mem='“Above all these put on love, which binds everything together in perfect harmony.” (Colossians 3:14)',
 assess='Ask students to name five virtues Paul lists and give an example for each.'),
'7-8-3': dict(
 idea='Revelation unveils Christ’s victory and the new creation. Its visions, full of symbols from the Old Testament, comfort persecuted Christians and show the heavenly liturgy. It ends with God dwelling among us and “Come, Lord Jesus!”',
 hook='Read Revelation 21:1–5 aloud. Ask: “What is missing in that city?” (tears, death, mourning, pain). Hope is the point of the book.',
 activity='“Final chapters” study (30 min). Read Revelation 21–22. Students list images (new Jerusalem, river of life, tree of life, no temple, light of the Lamb) and trace each to the Old Testament (Genesis, Ezekiel, Isaiah). Then link to the Mass (Holy, Holy, Holy; the Lamb of God).',
 teach=['Revelation is apocalyptic literature, written to encourage the persecuted: the Lamb has conquered (CCC 1042–1050). Its symbols (numbers, beasts, colors) are not a timetable for the end.',
        'The heavenly liturgy (Revelation 4–5) is the model of the Mass; we join the angels and saints in worship (CCC 1137–1139). The “new Jerusalem” is the Church in glory.',
        'The final prayer “Come, Lord Jesus” (22:20) is the cry of the Church; “Maranatha” is prayed in the Eucharist (CCC 1130).',
        'Warn gently against date-setting and fear-based interpretations. The Church teaches that no one knows the day or hour (CCC 673).'],
 misc=['“Revelation predicts exact dates and events.”', '“Revelation is about fear.”'],
 mem='“Behold, I am making all things new.” (Revelation 21:5)',
 assess='Ask students to explain Revelation’s purpose and give two images of the new creation.'),
}
